  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nHigh pressure continues across much of the region through \ntonight. A few showers popped up late this pm/early this evening\nover the portions of the central-eastern Mohawk Valley with the\nweak backdoor front becoming stationary. Some slight chance \nPoPs were added for a few hours from 7-9 pm and Johnstown in the\nNYS mesonet picked up 0.01\". \n\nLow temperatures tonight into tomorrow morning continue to be \nnear normal with mostly cloudy conditions. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nAfternoon Update: The forecast remains on track for tomorrow night \ninto Thursday morning. Increased wind gusts to range between 15 to \n30 mph for this timeframe as well as winds above the surface have \ngood potential to mix down and a breezy day is in store for \nThursday. See previous discussion below for more details. \n\nPrevious Discussion: After the recent stretch of dry weather, \nunsettled conditions are expected to arrive Wed P.M into early \nThu as a progressive upper level trough and associated cold \nfront move across the region. Initially thhrough the day on Wed,\nshowers will be confined to areas north/west of Albany, closer \nto where the better forcing will be. Elsewhere, clouds will \nthicken and humidity levels will increase, as dewpoints rise \ninto the 60s.\n\nShowers are expected to then spread south/east across the rest \nof the area Wed night into early Thu A.M. as the cold front \nmoves east across the area. Due to increasing moisture (PWAT \nanomalies rise to +1 to +3 STDEV) some embedded downpours will \nlikely occur. Due to the progressive movement of the system and \nfairly strong winds aloft, flooding is not anticipated. Guidance\ncontinues to indicate little instability with MUCAPE < 500 J/Kg\nWed evening/night for areas north/west of Albany. Will continue\nto mention slight/low chance of thunder, but risk for severe \nstorms remains low. Due to strong flow aloft, SPC continues to \nindicate a Marginal Risk (level 1 of 5) of severe storms in the \nMohawk Valley and SW Adirondacks. This seems like a worst-case \nscenario though due to limited instability along with \nunfavorable time at night. NCAR AI NWP convective hazards \nforecast continues to show very minimal probs for any severe \nweather at < 5%. With strengthening SW winds aloft Wed into Wed \nnight (40-45 kt at 850 mb), occasional gusts of 20-30 mph will \noccur in favored S-N oriented valleys and southern facing \nslopes. \n\nAs the cold front pushes through mainly Thu morning, winds will\nshift to the W-NW with more widespread gusts of 20-30 mph \nexpected in a developing deeper mixing environment with cold \nadvection commencing. It will still be warm due to the mixing, \nalthough humidity levels will start to drop from NW to SE late \nin the day. A few lingering showers may occur along the front, \nespecially south of Albany.",
